{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/connector at 46ccc0d6321055e898e4a27cb14533d92694a668 on request/github-connector-render. Successor to 4f23a8fa0a3de04dec05fd37addf517ac07ea4ec.\n\nReverts the staleness widening carried by the two predecessors, and closes a hole neither of them saw.\n\nThe widening rested on a false premise. I argued that a replacement charter must cite what it replaces, so every correctly replaced charter is stale by construction and refusing on staleness would leave no charter an operator could state. Codex checked it against the log and I verified it: charter b86860f2 does not rest on its predecessor at all, only mentions it in prose. Its single basis is a request that is retired — a request I retired myself earlier the same day. The staleness I treated as a law of the system was the consequence of my own act, and correct replacement produces no such thing: a successor rests on stable current governance while the separate supersession names it as an additional basis, which is the pattern already required here for artifact succession.\n\nThe security argument stands on its own regardless. A stale basis may be exactly the scope an operator withdrew, so admitting under it turns a flare into continuing authority for an irreversible public write, and a warning printed after admission re-authorizes nothing and asks nobody to acknowledge anything. Both the charter gate and clause admission now refuse, and each refusal names which fact applied and what repair it wants.\n\nThe hole: admission never established that a clause belonged to the charter the run acts under. Operator standing on the author says the actor may state clauses somewhere, not that this clause was granted under this doorstep, so a clause written under another charter or under one since withdrawn was honoured beneath whichever charter the run named — widening admission without stating anything. Clauses are now admitted only when their signed bases reach the acting charter, following the chain rather than requiring a direct citation, and an unnamed charter anchors nothing so a forgotten flag admits nothing rather than everything.\n\nKept from the reverted attempt: the named refusals and the distinction between nothing stated and something refused. Neither needed a looser door.\n\nEvery refusal is mutation-checked: dropping the charter anchor admits a foreign clause and one with no charter at all, and restoring either widening fails the tests that pin it.\n\ngo vet and the full suite pass, and the worktree is clean at this head.","body":{"branch":"request/github-connector-render","commit":"46ccc0d6321055e898e4a27cb14533d92694a668","head":"46ccc0d6321055e898e4a27cb14533d92694a668","path":"internal/connector"}}